; Every node's span, read back out of the source it points at.
;
; `span_dump` prints one line per node in `test-parser/main.zn`: the node's kind
; and the text between its span's two positions. That output is checked in as
; `main.spans`, and this rule fails when the two differ.
;
; What it is for: a span is only ever wrong by pointing somewhere, and the rest
; of the suite cannot see where. The acceptance tests ask whether a file parses,
; and the grouping tests ask which shape it parsed to -- neither reads a
; position. So a grammar change that widens a span to swallow a binder, or
; narrows one past its own bracket, passes everything and is noticed only in
; review, if at all.
;
; A failing diff is not by itself a bug: a change to the grammar or to the
; fixture is expected to move spans. Read the diff, satisfy yourself that each
; moved span still covers what its node stands for, and promote it with
; `dune promote`.
(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  main.spans.actual
  (run %{exe:../tools/span_dump.exe} %{dep:../test-parser/main.zn})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff main.spans main.spans.actual)))
